---
import { withBase } from '@lib/url';
import { SITE, AUTHOR, PAPER, absolute } from '@lib/seo';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LaneInterleave from '../components/LaneInterleave.astro';

const docs = (await getCollection('docs')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

const description =
  'vphilox is a header-only C++20 library built on Philox4x32-10. A checkpoint is six ' +
  '32-bit integers and a word offset, so a stream survives a move between standard ' +
  'libraries, instruction sets and thread counts.';

// Two entities on the home page: the site itself, and the library it documents.
const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebSite',
    name: SITE.name,
    alternateName: SITE.tagline,
    url: absolute(),
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: AUTHOR.name, url: AUTHOR.orcid },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
    name: SITE.name,
    description,
    url: absolute(),
    codeRepository: SITE.repo,
    programmingLanguage: 'C++',
    runtimePlatform: 'C++20',
    license: ['https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT', 'https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0'],
    author: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: AUTHOR.name,
      identifier: AUTHOR.orcid,
      sameAs: AUTHOR.orcid,
    },
    keywords: [
      'pseudorandom number generation',
      'counter-based PRNG',
      'Philox',
      'SIMD',
      'AVX2',
      'AVX-512',
      'ARM NEON',
      'C++20',
      'reproducibility',
    ],
    citation: { '@type': 'CreativeWork', name: PAPER.title, url: PAPER.doiUrl },
  },
];
---

<Base title={SITE.name} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <h1 class="hero">Portable, seekable random streams for parallel CPU simulation</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    <strong>vphilox</strong> is a header-only C++20 library built on Philox4x32-10, a
    counter-based generator whose whole state is a key and a position. A checkpoint is
    six 32-bit integers and a word offset. It survives a move between standard
    libraries, instruction sets, and thread counts. <code>std::mt19937</code> survives
    none of the three.
  </p>

  <LaneInterleave />

  <h2>The objection was always speed</h2>

  <p>
    An earlier attempt to put Philox into a production machine-learning library was
    rejected on the grounds that scalar Philox runs at one tenth the speed of the
    Mersenne Twister. That tenfold penalty did not reproduce on any of the five
    processors measured here. Interleaving independent counters across SIMD lanes puts
    the generator between 0.22&times; and 0.75&times; the cost per byte of
    <code>std::mt19937</code>, so on the bulk path it is faster than the engine it
    replaces. Aggregate cost per byte is flat across thread counts, and the first
    parallel limit the paper finds is hyperthread co-location, not the generator.
  </p>

  <dl class="stats">
    <div>
      <dt>0.22–0.75&times;</dt>
      <dd>cost per byte against <code>std::mt19937</code>, bulk path, five processors</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>7 integers</dt>
      <dd>the entire serialized state, version tag aside</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>O(1)</dt>
      <dd><code>discard(N)</code> costs the same for <em>N</em> = 1 and <em>N</em> = 2<sup>40</sup></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <h2>What actually gets written</h2>

  <p>
    The C++ standard fixes the text format of an engine&rsquo;s stream operators only
    partially, and the three major standard libraries disagree. libstdc++ writes the 624
    state words of <code>std::mt19937</code> in raw internal order followed by a position
    index; libc++ and the Microsoft standard library write the same words rotated into
    canonical order and write no position at all. The word counts agree, which is what
    makes the failure dangerous: a reader handed 624 integers cannot tell that they are
    in the wrong order, so the restore succeeds, at a position nobody chose, without a
    diagnostic.
  </p>

  <p>vphilox writes a position instead.</p>

  <pre><code>vphilox1 &lt;k0&gt; &lt;k1&gt; &lt;c0&gt; &lt;c1&gt; &lt;c2&gt; &lt;c3&gt; &lt;word&gt;</code></pre>

  <p class="note">
    Seven decimal integers and a version tag. Nothing about the engine&rsquo;s refill
    buffer is in there, which is why states written before the AVX-512 kernel raised the
    refill size from 8 blocks to 16 still load after it. Digits are formatted and parsed
    by hand, so an imbued grouping locale cannot corrupt a checkpoint, and unrecognised
    input is refused rather than interpreted.
  </p>

  <nav class="cards">
    <a class="card" href={withBase("paper")}>
      <span class="card-kind">Paper</span>
      <span class="card-title">Portable, Seekable Random Streams for Parallel CPU Simulation</span>
      <span class="card-note">Full text, figures and tables &middot; also <em>PDF</em></span>
    </a>
    {docs.map((d) => (
      <a class="card" href={withBase(`docs/${d.id}`)}>
        <span class="card-kind">Explainer</span>
        <span class="card-title">{d.data.title}</span>
        <span class="card-note">{d.data.description}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </nav>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); }

  /* The figure is the page's opening argument, so it gets more air than the
     2.75rem a figure carries in running text. */
  .lede + :global(.anim) { margin: 2.5rem 0 3.5rem; }

  /* Marked out by rules and type, not boxed: the same treatment the tables in
     the paper get. The numbers are the one place colour is spent on the page. */
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem 2rem;
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
    padding: 1.4rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }
  .stats div { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; align-content: start; }
  .stats dt {
    font-family: var(--font-head), system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-head), system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  /* Follows a code block rather than a paragraph, so it takes no indent. */
  .note { text-indent: 0; margin-top: 1.15rem; }

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
  .card {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.2rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 5px;
    background: var(--bg-sunk);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    transition: background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .card:hover { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-sunk) 60%, var(--rule)); }
  .card:hover .card-title { color: var(--link); }
  .card-kind {
    font-family: var(--font-head), system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .card-title {
    font-family: var(--font-head), system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .card-note { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--text-soft); }
</style>
